<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The extension's entitlements, for local development.

  WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY

  `com.apple.security.application-groups` is *provisioning-profile-backed*: Xcode refuses
  to build a target that declares one unless a real team and a matching profile are
  selected, and App Groups are not available on a free Apple ID at all. Declaring it here
  is what produced

      "UnpluggedAU" requires a provisioning profile. Enable development signing and
      select a provisioning profile in the Signing & Capabilities editor.

  Requiring a paid membership before the plugin can be built once is a bad trade, so the
  group lives in UnpluggedAU-Signed.entitlements and is used by the `Signed` configuration.

  WHAT REPLACES IT

  The sandbox stays on — it costs nothing under ad-hoc signing, and it is what the shipped
  build will run under, so developing without it would hide sandbox bugs until the worst
  possible moment. The temporary exception below gives the extension read access to the one
  directory it needs: where the standalone app keeps its projects. Read-only, because the
  plugin only ever reads them — the app owns that directory.

  A temporary exception is *not* profile-backed, so ad-hoc signing grants it. The path is
  relative to the real home directory, not this extension's container, and must stay in
  step with `sharedDataDirectory()` in UnpluggedAudioUnit.swift and with
  `home_data_dir()` in src-tauri/src/shared_container.rs. The trailing slash matters: it
  is what makes the exception cover the directory's contents.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.home-relative-path.read-only</key>
	<array>
		<string>/Library/Application Support/Unplugged/</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
